# SkillPath environment template.
#
# Copy to .env.local and fill in. .env.local is gitignored; this file is not,
# so it must never contain a real value.

# Supabase dashboard -> Project Settings -> API Keys.
# Project URL, e.g. https://abcdefghijklm.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=

# The publishable (anon) key: sb_publishable_... or a JWT starting eyJ...
#
# Public by design. It no longer opens anything: RLS is enabled on every table
# and select/insert/update/delete are revoked from `anon`, so this key is
# refused at the grant layer. Nothing in the app reads through it any more —
# it is kept because @supabase/ssr wants a key and because the e2e harness
# still records which project it belongs to.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# The service_role key: SECRET. Same dashboard page, the one behind "Reveal".
#
# NO LONGER WHAT ORDINARY REQUESTS QUERY WITH. createClient() in
# lib/supabase/server.ts is built on the ANON key plus the caller's session, so
# `auth.uid()` is set and the RLS policies in
# supabase/migrations/*_securitate_rls.sql actually apply. That was the point of
# the security migration: with a service-role client on every request, the whole
# policy set would be enabled on the tables and enforced against nobody.
#
# This key is still needed, for createServiceClient() — the two jobs RLS makes
# impossible on purpose: the admin question bank (which has to read
# `answers.is_correct`) and cross-user aggregates on the admin overview. Every
# caller of it sits behind assertAdmin().
#
# Two rules follow from it bypassing RLS, and neither is optional:
#
#   1. No NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, ever. That prefix is what decides whether Next
#      inlines a value into the browser bundle.
#   2. lib/supabase/server.ts imports 'server-only', so importing it from a
#      client component fails the build rather than shipping this key.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The AI features (Epic 9). ARCHITECTURE §6.
#
# NO VENDOR IS NAMED HERE ANY MORE. lib/ai used to hold an Anthropic-specific
# provider built on that vendor's SDK; it now speaks the OpenAI-compatible
# `POST /chat/completions` shape, which every hosted API worth naming and every
# local runtime also speaks. Choosing a vendor is the three variables below.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Which implementation lib/ai/provider.ts hands out: `mock` or `openai`.
#
# Unset means `mock`, and that is the right default everywhere except a
# deployment you are demoing from. The mock returns deterministic fixtures, so
# a broken API key never blocks a teammate and CI never spends money — all three
# CI workflows pin AI_PROVIDER=mock explicitly. An unknown value warns and falls
# back to `mock` rather than throwing at import.
#
# `openai` and `openai-compatible` are the same implementation. The name says
# which PROTOCOL is spoken, not who is on the other end — that is AI_BASE_URL.
AI_PROVIDER=mock

# The master switch, separate from the choice of provider.
#
# Unset means enabled. `false`, `0`, `off` or `no` means no provider is called
# at all and every AI surface renders its rule-based text — SP-093 AC3, and the
# thing to flip if the model starts saying something you would rather it did
# not five minutes before a demo.
# AI_ENABLED=true

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The three that decide which model answers. All required when AI_PROVIDER is
# not `mock`; readAiConfig() refuses to start otherwise and names the one that
# is missing, rather than degrading to rule-based text and letting you wonder.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Where to POST. No trailing slash needed — one is stripped either way, because
# `//chat/completions` is routed by some gateways and 404ed by others.
#
#   OpenAI      https://api.openai.com/v1
#   Groq        https://api.groq.com/openai/v1
#   OpenRouter  https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
#   Together    https://api.together.xyz/v1
#   DeepSeek    https://api.deepseek.com/v1
#   Mistral     https://api.mistral.ai/v1
#   Ollama      http://localhost:11434/v1      (local; AI_API_KEY can be junk)
#   LM Studio   http://localhost:1234/v1       (local; same)
#
# Plain http is allowed on purpose — refusing it would rule out every local
# runtime, which is half the reason this provider is generic.
AI_BASE_URL=

# SECRET. Sent as `Authorization: Bearer`.
#
# No NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, ever — that prefix is what decides whether Next
# inlines a value into the browser bundle. lib/ai/openai-compatible.ts imports
# 'server-only' and calls assertServerSide(), so a client import fails the build
# and, if it somehow got past that, fails at runtime too.
#
# Surrounding quotes and a trailing newline are stripped on read, because both
# are what actually happens when somebody pastes a key, and both otherwise
# arrive as a 401 that reads like a revoked key.
AI_API_KEY=

# The model id, exactly as the endpoint above spells it. There is no default:
# a guessed model is a 404 on every single call.
#
#   gpt-4o-mini · llama-3.3-70b-versatile · deepseek-chat · mistral-small-latest
#   qwen2.5:7b (Ollama) · anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.5 (OpenRouter)
AI_MODEL=

# Whether to send `response_format: {"type":"json_object"}` on the one call
# whose answer is a JSON object.
#
# Unset means yes, which is right for nearly every backend. Set it to `false`
# for one that rejects the unknown field with a 400 — a few partial
# implementations do. The prompt asks for JSON in words either way, and the
# provider strips a ```json fence before parsing, so turning this off costs
# reliability rather than function.
# AI_JSON_MODE=true

# How hard the model thinks before it answers. Unset sends nothing.
#
# `reasoning_effort` is the OpenAI protocol's field, so no vendor is named here
# either. What it buys is LATENCY, not shape: a reasoning model spends these
# tokens before the first token of the answer, AI_*_MAX_TOKENS does not bound
# them, and none of them come back.
#
# The plan call is the one that notices, being the longest of the three. On a
# model that thinks by default it measured 16s against a 10s AI_TIMEOUT_MS —
# so every generation was aborted, and every plan rendered its rule text with
# no error anywhere to say why. `none` brought the same call back in 5s.
#
#   none · minimal · low · medium · high    (anything else warns and is dropped)
#
# Set it low on a model that reasons by default: none of these three prompts is
# asked to solve anything, only to write about what the rules already decided.
#
# NOT EVERY MODEL ACCEPTS EVERY VALUE, and the refusal is unhelpful — a bare
# `400 Request contains an invalid argument`, naming no field. `none` is the
# one that gets refused; `minimal` has been accepted everywhere it was tried.
# So if all three AI features start failing the moment this is set, lift the
# value one step before suspecting anything else.
# AI_REASONING_EFFORT=minimal

# SESSION_SECRET is GONE. It signed `skillpath_session`, the application's own
# HMAC session cookie, and lib/auth/session.ts is deleted: the session is
# Supabase's now, in `sb-<project-ref>-auth-token`, signed with a key that never
# leaves the project. Nothing reads SESSION_SECRET — remove it from .env.local
# and from the repository secrets when convenient.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# .env.e2e — the end-to-end run (SP-101). A SEPARATE FILE, not this one.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `npm run test:e2e` registers members, grades papers and writes plan rows, so
# it needs a Supabase project of its own. The names above again, with the
# TEST project's values:
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
#   SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=
#
# The service_role key is not optional. RLS is on in the test project and the
# anon role satisfies no policy, so the harness reads an empty database and
# reports it as unseeded — "the E2E project has no category 0" about a project
# that has one. The seed hits the same wall from the other side.
#
# e2e/helpers/env.ts compares BOTH the url and the service_role key against
# .env.local and refuses to run if either matches. Seed the project once with
# `npm run seed:e2e`; the baseline needs its twenty questions or no run can open.
#
# In CI there is no file — the same four names come from repository secrets.


